= Conclusions

== What this example covered

The preceding chapters exercised each feature the template provides: chapter
openings with and without an abbreviated running head, figures with separate
legends, panel references, tables, numbered equations, raw code blocks,
citations in both superscript and baseline form, and cross-references into the
appendix.

== Before you submit

Formatting the manuscript is only part of meeting the requirements. The
template cannot check the following for you, so work through them yourself:

- Confirm the compiled PDF has no copyright page and no signature page. Axess
  inserts both. Failing to remove them is the most common formatting mistake.
- Confirm the first physically numbered page carries the right numeral, and that
  Arabic numbering restarts at 1 at this chapter rather than continuing the
  Roman sequence.
- Check that every figure is legible in print as well as on screen, is at least
  72 dpi and preferably 150 dpi, and does not exceed the page size.
- Move very large or high-resolution graphics out of the main file and submit
  them as supplemental files instead.
- Keep the main file under 100 MB. Supplemental files may total up to twenty
  files of 1 GB each.
- Remove any embedded audio, video, or animation from the body.
- Apply no password protection or encryption to the PDF.
- Check that the title contains no formulas, symbols, superscripts, subscripts,
  or Greek letters, and that the month and year on the title page are the month
  and year you actually submit.
- Name the file using only alphanumeric characters, hyphens, underscores, `@`,
  spaces, ampersands, and commas, at most 120 characters, not beginning with a
  space, period, underscore, or hyphen.

== A closing note

Previously approved dissertations are not a reliable guide to current
formatting, because the guidelines change. Neither is this template. Read the
requirements yourself before you submit, and ask the Registrar if anything is
ambiguous — a non-conforming submission has to be redesigned and resubmitted,
which can delay degree conferral.
